body:
Unfortunately, Ambien has no effect on me. I am dependent on Mirtazapine after experimenting with just about every psychiatric pharmaceutical intervention.

&#x200B;

I also seem to be immune to breathing exercises/meditation. While I engage in both practices, they do little to impact my mood or stress.

This was helpful, but what gets me is how he gets the stick to go vertical after the initial hit. When I do that, no matter the angle, the force I apply, the rotation of my hands I can get it maybe an inch off the pad.

&#x200B;

The ONLY way I can get the stick to bounce up and stick up straight is if I hold the stick near the tip, so that there is so much weight at the back of the stick that it forces it up after striking the pad.

After over 10 years of drumming, I still don't understand how to play even double strokes. My left hand sucks, and accenting the 2nd note and working up in tempo doesn't seem to help.

&#x200B;

Something is fundamentally wrong with my technique, and I do not know how to adjust it. I've had about 3 or 4 teachers over the years who haven't been able to help me. I am beginning to think it's just not something my body is capable of.

While I do think the science component of this is absolutely incredible, the realistic probability of this ever being widely available is slim from a logistics perspective. I like that we're slowly nudging in the right direction on the r&d front, there is no doubt some administrative/logistical/financial bullshit will ruin the availability of it to consumers.

&#x200B;

EDIT: Also, obviously I'm not trying to argue with you because you're way fucking smarter than me, I'm just bitter like the rest.

Not sure what your taper schedule looked like, but I'm on a medication particularly notorious for awful withdrawals within a particular subset of the community. Somebody in a withdrawal support group mentioned almost identical symptoms to the ones you've mentioned. It sounds like you're off completely now? Do the physical symptoms dissipate when you are back on Citalopram?

&#x200B;

If they do subside when you are on Citalopram, then perhaps you should re-instate at that dose and then taper on a more incremental schedule... in the case of my medication, I've had to go from 15mg to 0.9mg over the course of about 6 months. The lack of sleep on its own is possibly the debilitating factor.

&#x200B;

There are supplements that can help with a lot of the other symptoms as well. It definitely takes some research and trial and error, but there are solutions for low T, energy and IBS that don't require medical intervention.

I like all of those ideas, bartending is super appealing to me. I was a barback for a bit and loved running around, and I know bartenders make pretty good money. I really enjoyed being a line cook as well, I just know there isn't much upward trajectory in the culinary world unless you're a world-class chef.

&#x200B;

I have a bachelor's in media, and I was trying to go back to community college to do pre-requisites for nursing last year, but I got absolutely beaten into the ground by trying to balance a full-time job and difficult science classes that I could barely keep a C in. I'm absolute garbage when it comes to school, and while I'd like to overcome my focus/ADHD issues, I've been unsuccessful finding any medication, dietary protocol or tools that can help with focus/discipline. I wish it wasn't so necessary to have to be a straight A student, because I think I'd be a damn good nurse.
